== Property-design rules ==

=== One property, one job ===

Do not overload one field with several dimensions merely because the values are text.

For example:

<pre>
Epistemic status = Established
Lifecycle status = Archived
Review status = Reviewed
</pre>

is meaningful. <code>Epistemic status = Archived</code> collapses evidentiary maturity and lifecycle.

=== Use the strongest honest semantic relation ===

If A implements B, use <code>Implements</code> rather than <code>Related to</code>. If the stronger claim cannot be justified, use the weaker relation.

=== Do not encode display-only data as ontology ===

Word counts, UI style, section order, decorative state, and other presentation concerns should not become semantic properties unless a real machine/query workflow needs them.

=== Avoid duplicated MediaWiki-native state ===

MediaWiki already stores revision time, editor, page title, namespace, and history. Do not duplicate native data into SMW unless there is a concrete query/interoperability reason.

=== Prefer relations over encoded strings ===

When the target is a real wiki knowledge object, a Page relation is usually more useful than embedding its title in free text.

=== Do not create properties from documentation alone ===

The V1 Property Dictionary proposed dozens of fields. Some encoded durable distinctions; some duplicated native data; some reflected one historical workflow; many were never authored in <code>Property:</code> namespace.

V2 requires a demonstrated use before promotion.

== V1 property families under review ==

Potential future families include:

* authorship / contribution;
* dates where MediaWiki-native revision dates are insufficient;
* external identifiers and interoperability mappings;
* method / evidence structure;
* location / participation;
* project goals and outputs;
* dataset schema/licensing;
* more specific temporal relationships.

These are not rejected. They should be added through real content work so their cardinality, type, and meaning are validated against use.

== Change process ==

For a new canonical property:

# show multiple real use cases;
# define one precise semantic job;
# choose the correct SMW type;
# specify allowed cardinality/constraints only where actually needed;
# test queries;
# document interaction with existing properties;
# migrate legacy values deliberately;
# add validation only after semantics are stable.

== Historical source ==

V1's [[BITwiki Property Dictionary]], [[SMW Property Definitions]], [[SMW Property Types Complete]], [[Epistemic Properties]], [[Relationship Types]], and related schema documents remain preserved in <code>archive-v1/</code>.

The exhaustive V1 implementation audit is important context: V1 documented a far larger property system than the four authored imported-vocabulary Property pages that actually existed. V2 intentionally makes implementation and documentation converge.

